WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.200
- The reason Taiwan knew about this was

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.160
because there were Taiwanese doctors

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.510
who had heard from their
mainland colleagues

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.780
that the medical staff in
Wuhan were getting sick.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.910
So that's how you know
human-to-human transmission

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.390
is occurring, it's when
it's not animal-to-person,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.680
but person-to-person, the patients

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.490
are transferring it to the doctors.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:25.487
So Taiwan reports this
and it takes weeks before,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.710
I think on the 14th of January,

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.530
you still have that
famous tweet by the WHO

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:34.920
saying there's still no evidence

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.620
of human-to-human transmission.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.297
Two weeks after Taiwan
told them that there was.

00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:42.964
(serious music)

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:51.610
- Hey, I'm Dave Rubin and
this is "The Rubin Report".

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:53.950
Reminder everybody, you can get all

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:57.540
of our full episodes absolutely ad free

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.800
five full days early at rubinreport.com.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.530
And, more importantly, joining me today

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.960
is the Managing Director
of Ideas Beyond Borders,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.470
as well as the New York
Editor for Spectator Magazine,

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.400
Melissa Chen, welcome
to "The Rubin Report".

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.460
- Hi Dave, thank you for having me.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.880
- I cannot believe that this is your

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.260
first solo appearance
on "The Rubin Report".

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:27.150
You were on the show way back
when, when we were on Aura TV.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.800
That's something like
20-25 years ago, I think,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.440
at this point, with our
friend Faisal Al Mutar,

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.792
who's your cohort at Ideas Beyond Borders.

00:01:35.792 --> 00:01:36.680
- Yes.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.950
- There's a ton I wanna
talk to you about of course,

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.230
but can you just very
quickly, who is Melissa Chen?

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.207
How did you get to "The Rubin Report"?

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.510
- Well I have to thank
you for having me on again

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:52.790
because that last time,
when I did it with Faisal,

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.810
I had the worst hair possible.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.810
I was, for some reason,
identifying as a male K-pop star

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:00.880
and I don't know what was going on.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.340
So I'm thankful to have
another chance to show

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.540
what it actually can
look like, potentially.

00:02:07.515 --> 00:02:08.520
So my name is Melissa.
- Yes, your hair's

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.103
looking very luxurious by the way.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.250
- Which is hard to do when
there's no barbers, okay?

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.753
So I'll put that out there.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.350
So my name's Melissa and I
now also am a journalist,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:26.690
I write for "Spectator",
which is a British magazine.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.530
And I also run a nonprofit organization

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.360
called Ideas Beyond Borders,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.400
together with Faisal Al
Mutar, who's a dear friend

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.300
of both me and Dave's for a long time.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.192
I grew up in Singapore.

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:42.090
So I only came to the
U.S. when I was about 17,

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.550
came for college.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.010
Loved the experience of living in a place

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:47.560
with so much civil liberties.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.650
And I guess I stayed after college.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.610
I have actually a background
in computational biology,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:57.490
that was my degree and, like
a good Asian or a bad Asian,

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.870
I guess, I'm not using my degree

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:04.410
and I ended up running
a nonprofit and writing.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.623
So that's how I got here.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.430
- Yeah, all right, very good.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:13.460
Let's just quickly
recap a little bit about

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.740
what you guys are doing
with Ideas Beyond Borders

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.680
and then I wanna focus more
on what you've been doing

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.770
related to Coronavirus,
some interesting stuff

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.000
in New York City trying
to get people supplies

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.670
and you have some knowledge
on what's going on

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:25.870
with China and the rest of it.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:27.030
But just tell everybody a little bit

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.820
about Ideas Beyond Borders.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.660
Because before Corona, there
was a whole other world

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.230
out there and you guys were doing

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.210
some really great stuff
and continue to do.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.910
- Right, I mean it's interesting

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:40.870
because early January of 2020,

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:42.960
the focus of this world
was on the Middle East,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.050
right after the whole General
Soleimani was killed in Iran

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.360
and that was shifting the focus
to that part of the world.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.740
It seemed like a hotspot.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.610
There were protests going
on in Lebanon and Iraq

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.710
and so that seemed like
it would be the start

00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:58.830
of World War III or something

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.320
and then all of a sudden Coronavirus,

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.550
it just consumed everything.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.850
So Ideas Beyond Borders is
a nonprofit organization

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.800
founded by my friend Faisal,
who is an Iraqi refugee.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.280
And what we do is we take ideas

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.240
that are not available in Arabic,

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.230
translate them into the language,

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.040
and make them freely available.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.940
We focus on ideas about
science, about philosophy,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.890
and about civil rights, pro
liberty ideas basically.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:28.790
And the idea is just if
you have a heteronormative,

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:30.963
heteronormative? Sorry, heterodox.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.170
If you're exposed to heterodox ideas,

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.050
that it would challenge peoples' minds

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.710
and it would open up dogmatic thinking.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.280
So that was the reason Ideas
Beyond Borders was founded.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.020
And actually today, not only
have we done a ton of books,

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.230
Wikipedia articles, we've also done videos

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:49.460
and right now we're actually combating

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.640
a lot of the misinformation
about Coronavirus in Arabic.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.460
Because ever since China
released that whole

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:58.253
propaganda theory about the thing about

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.710
the US army was the one
that released the virus,

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.040
that's the kind of conspiracy theories

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.490
that take root in the Arab world,

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:09.410
that's what they wanna believe.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.280
Yeah, all right, so we're
obviously gonna focus

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.410
a lot on that but, very quickly,

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:14.030
you mentioned that you
grew up in Singapore

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:15.560
until you were 16, 17 years old.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.640
Can you just talk a little
bit about what that was like

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.370
and why that kinda led
you to being someone

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.460
that actually cares about
freedom of expression

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.100
and open inquiry and all that good liberty

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.920
stuff that you talk about?

00:05:29.432 --> 00:05:31.750
- I mean, Singapore's a
great place to grow up.

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:35.440
In a way, it's very stable,
very safe and very affluent

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.510
as a country, very, very
high GDP per capita.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:40.490
But the one thing that we didn't have

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:42.810
was political freedoms.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.130
So Singapore's ranked pretty low in terms

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:48.560
of freedom of the press, freedom of speech

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.550
and also very basic things,

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.750
like there is absolutely
no freedom to assemble.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.210
So if you decided to
go into a public square

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:01.080
and hold a sign that said
something like, I don't know,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.957
"Impeach the Prime Minister"
or something, or even just

00:06:03.957 --> 00:06:07.820
"Against the Death Penalty",
you would be arrested

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:12.130
because you didn't get a
permit for the protest.

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:16.237
And that's the kind of
very ordered society

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.860
that it has created.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.630
But for me it's always
kind of been this thing

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.340
on my shoulder, kind
of weighed down on me,

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.940
that you couldn't just speak your mind,

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:32.860
you always had to censor
and I knew I wanted

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.700
to be in a place where
this was just not even,

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.443
I didn't have to think about,
that you could just be free,

00:06:39.443 --> 00:06:43.149
be free to be yourself, so that's why I

00:06:43.149 --> 00:06:44.324
chose the United States.
- Yeah, how are we doing

00:06:44.324 --> 00:06:46.698
with that in America these days?

00:06:46.698 --> 00:06:50.030
- (laughs) You know
what? Still a lot better.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:55.030
But it's heartbreaking to hear and see

00:06:56.092 --> 00:07:01.092
that these freedoms
are slowly being eroded

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.960
and really we were doing it to ourselves.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.080
And it may not be coming
from the governments

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.470
but when you have this kind of mob rule

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:13.200
and cancel culture, it
is a defacto censorship

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.560
and it ends up having similar
effects on your psyche,

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.420
on society as a whole
and it kinda bubbles up.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:26.220
But it's still very different,
I would have to say.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.010
There's a difference
between getting disappeared

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:30.740
by your government and getting
jailed by your government

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:31.650
for saying the wrong thing

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:35.210
than it is lose your job but still sucks.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:36.360
- Yeah, for sure.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.100
So let's talk a bit about corona and China

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:41.560
and some of the things
that you've been doing.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:45.910
So, first I wanna start, you
had a tweet thread recently

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:48.050
that you found a donor who wanted to put

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.280
a whole bunch of money to get

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:51.830
doctors and nurses
- He was a donor

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:52.733
of my organization.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:55.810
- Yeah, and he wanted to put money

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:57.850
in so the doctors and
nurses could get masks.

00:07:57.850 --> 00:07:59.630
That's sort of the short version of it

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:03.230
and suddenly you found
out that there is a lot

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:05.610
of red tape to make what should be

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.507
simple things happen actually happen.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:12.770
- Right, right, so this what they call

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:16.640
personal protective
equipment, short would be PPE,

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:19.290
it includes masks, gowns, the face shields

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.820
and everything that healthcare workers

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:23.970
really need to use to protect themselves

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:27.680
and a lot of people had been talking about

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.960
okay, we're in dire need of
this, we didn't stockpile,

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.100
a lot of the hospitals didn't stockpile,

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:35.880
the states didn't do it.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.750
And so when you have
a sudden rush of cases

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:42.253
as you did in New York City,
the ICUs were over-capacitated,

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:46.200
that's when hospitals realize that,

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.790
oh no, we're running out
of PPEs very quickly,

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:51.590
we're just going through
them at such a quick rate,

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:53.090
and it was a huge shortage.

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:54.520
And it's not just in the United States,

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.060
it's pretty much anywhere else.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.810
Italy's facing the same problem as well.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:01.420
So a very, very kind donor reached out

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.576
and said, "I really wanna help."

00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:06.020
And he's not the only one.
There are a lot of people that,

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:07.873
it's one of the best things about America,

00:09:07.873 --> 00:09:12.350
is this ingenuity from just average people

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:15.484
who when there's a
problem, they just step up

00:09:15.484 --> 00:09:18.350
and wanna just contribute somehow.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:21.160
And so what I started finding was,

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:26.160
okay, I got the hospital saying
we need the PPE so badly,

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:30.180
healthcare workers are
saying the same thing

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.120
and then donors are coming forward.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.640
But then to even make that match happen

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.900
and get the masks to the
people that need them

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:42.080
was a complete Kafkaesque nightmare.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.270
And it's in part due to all the different

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:47.820
layers of regulations,
starting with the employers

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:51.500
basically said if you talk to the press

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:52.880
or if you post about it on social media,

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:55.160
you might get fired. And I
think there was a case where

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:57.460
a doctor in Washington State was fired

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:01.490
for basically blabbering
about the shortage.

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:04.460
And then you have the CDC and the WHO

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:07.365
saying, "You know what,
masks are not needed."

00:10:07.365 --> 00:10:10.530
This was some rhetoric
that's been circulating

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.980
since the crisis started.

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.980
And that helps hospitals
kind of CYA, cover your ass,

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:20.290
because if masks are not
needed, then the onus

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:23.283
isn't on them to stock up
and protect the workers.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.820
It basically protects them,
it gives them some liability

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:30.380
if, let's say you die
on the job or something,

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:34.133
then it's harder to sue
because it's not required,

00:10:36.281 --> 00:10:39.420
the CDC just said you
don't really need masks.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:40.870
And then you have compliance issues,

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:45.523
so the FDA, they need
to certify all the masks

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:50.220
for does it do what it says
it does and everything.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.640
But then different
layers of certification,

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:56.030
different agencies have
different standards

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:59.160
and then you have this bidding war,

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:03.040
so all 50 states are
bidding, the feds are bidding

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:07.410
for the same supply pool,
and then you have NGOs

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:08.810
and then you have the global market

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.210
and we all just end up
bidding against each other

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:12.710
and just driving the price up.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:18.020
What should have been done is perhaps

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.830
start of with some sort
of all the governors

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:22.860
come together at the start of consortium

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:26.880
and bid against global actors as well

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:31.880
and there's just so much
redundancy (microphone distorts)

00:11:32.335 --> 00:11:34.440
this entire market but
it's been so difficult

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:38.603
trying to get PPEs to the hospitals.

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:41.300
And the last
- Yeah, so I know

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:44.950
that lot of my audience hearing this

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:47.107
is gonna go, "Oh yeah, well of course,

00:11:47.107 --> 00:11:49.417
"regulation, red tape, this is exactly

00:11:49.417 --> 00:11:51.067
"what the government does to get us all

00:11:51.067 --> 00:11:53.687
"in a boondoggle where prices drive up,

00:11:53.687 --> 00:11:56.447
"nobody can get what they
want and the rest of it."

00:11:57.302 --> 00:11:59.870
So is the best way to just
get rid of all the red tape?

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:02.170
How do we then make sure that products

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.510
that actually work are
the ones getting to us,

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:07.230
if there's no regulation whatsoever?

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:09.965
I'm slightly playing the devil's advocate.

00:12:09.965 --> 00:12:11.430
- Well, there's short-term,
long-term issues.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:15.600
So one of the stories that came out

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:19.260
was this Brooklyn man
was apparently hoarding,

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:21.440
he had started purchasing
millions of masks

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:23.690
or something and was
hoarding a lot of them.

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:26.450
And we're so concerned about price gouging

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:29.360
that if you're an importer here,

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.130
you don't wanna be
guilty of price gouging,

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.400
so you don't import the masks

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.160
because of the price fluctuations

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.770
and the feds are gonna come after you,

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:39.600
as they did after this Brooklyn man,

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:41.100
who basically did nothing wrong

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.930
except buy a ton of masks
and hold on to them.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:47.283
That's his property and they
came and they seized it.

00:12:49.011 --> 00:12:50.661
And while this is good intention,

00:12:51.821 --> 00:12:53.900
businesses or people shouldn't be trying

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:55.963
to make a profit during a crisis,

00:12:56.830 --> 00:13:00.980
the problem is that the
price, when it goes up,

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:04.150
is basically an incentive
for produces to produce.

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:07.320
So we should be letting the
prices fluctuate, right,

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.800
because then we can
rush to meet the demand.

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:12.930
And it's one of those
policies or sentiments

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:14.860
that just has unintended consequences.

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:15.990
It sounds good in principle

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:19.003
but it has unintended consequences.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:24.140
I think the short term is we might need to

00:13:25.725 --> 00:13:27.260
be a bit more protectionist.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:29.930
I think it makes sense
to have export controls,

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:32.700
to prevent our current supply of PPEs

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:35.053
leaving the country,
we should've done that.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:38.310
But then in the long term,
we have to let the prices,

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:41.410
we have to let the market fluctuate

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:42.680
because it's gonna correct itself.

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:45.020
You know what Facebook did?

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:47.730
Facebook prevented mask advertisements

00:13:49.114 --> 00:13:51.440
because they were concerned
about the hoarding issue.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:53.273
So they basically said, "All right

00:13:53.273 --> 00:13:55.540
(microphone distorts) now"

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.727
But then if you (microphone distorts)

00:13:57.727 --> 00:14:01.610
and you came up with a
way to 3D print a mask,

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.730
now you (microphone distorts)
money for a Kickstarter.

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:07.080
So we're really shooting
ourselves in the foot.

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:11.940
- So you mention the WHO, the
World Health Organization,

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:14.620
and the CDC, the Center
for Disease Control.

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:16.990
It seems like in the
last month, the messaging

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:20.210
out of the WHO specifically has been just

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.340
absolutely all over the place.

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:23.757
And there's some people that say,

00:14:23.757 --> 00:14:25.720
"Oh, well they didn't want
to push people into masks"

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:27.370
sort of for the reasons you're saying

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:30.760
because then regular people
who maybe don't need them

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:32.070
are gonna get all the masks,

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:35.560
the people who need them
won't have access to them.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:37.390
But have they as an organization

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:40.513
really just kind of flubbed
this thing from the beginning?

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:43.043
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:43.928 --> 00:14:46.220
There's no question that the WHO

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:51.220
has basically abandoned
its duties in so many ways.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:54.903
I mean, you can go all
the way back to December.

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:58.870
If you go back to December 30th,

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:02.743
that's when China first
reported to the WHO

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:08.760
that there were these cases,
a cluster of about 41 people,

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.673
that was the first time it was reported.

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:15.083
A day later, on the 31st of
January, it was New Year's Eve,

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:19.447
Taiwan basically said to the WHO,

00:15:19.447 --> 00:15:23.187
"Hey, we have heard that
this is really serious

00:15:23.187 --> 00:15:25.960
"and that's there's
human-to-human transmission."

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:28.530
This was on the 31st of December

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:30.670
and the reason Taiwan knew about this

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:32.780
was because there were Taiwanese doctors

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:35.140
who had heard from their
mainland colleagues

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:38.410
that the medical staff in
Wuhan were getting sick.

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:40.050
So that's how you know human-to-human

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:41.350
transmission is occurring,

00:15:42.479 --> 00:15:45.790
it's not animal-to-person
but person-to-person,

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:49.120
the patients are transferring
it to the doctors.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:54.117
So Taiwan reports this
and it takes weeks before,

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:57.330
I think on the 14th of January,

00:15:57.330 --> 00:16:00.217
you still have that
famous tweet by the WHO

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:03.550
saying there's still no evidence

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:05.260
of human-to-human transmission,

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:09.440
two weeks after Taiwan
told them that there was.

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:14.440
And this has been the story
of how they've handled this.

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:17.073
They've Kowtow to China.

00:16:17.073 --> 00:16:19.280
- Well let's just pause
there for one sec though,

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:21.093
let's just pause there
for one sec. What is that?

00:16:21.093 --> 00:16:22.680
Like what do you think structurally,

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:25.340
as someone that knows about
organizations like this,

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:27.240
what happened in those two weeks?

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:29.110
Were they intentionally misleading us?

00:16:29.110 --> 00:16:31.010
Did they just completely drop the ball?

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:32.340
Was there political pressure?

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:34.580
Was it they didn't wanna be too alarmist?

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:36.873
Is it some combination
of all of those thing?

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:41.120
- No, actually, the problem is that Taiwan

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:43.760
doesn't have membership to the WHO.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:47.240
So China has very successfully
muscled Taiwan out.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:50.400
So Taiwan's not recognized as a member

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:53.160
and for Taiwan that's very dangerous

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:56.508
because if you don't have that connection

00:16:56.508 --> 00:16:59.210
to the WHO, you are not plugged into

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:01.670
this global health alert system.

00:17:01.670 --> 00:17:03.420
And the global health alert system

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:05.070
is not plugged into Taiwan.

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:08.860
So whatever intel Taiwan
had was completely missed.

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:11.100
And then the worst part for Taiwan

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:15.090
is that because China
basically muscled them out,

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:18.920
when all the other countries
were placing travel bans,

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:21.780
they were trying to ban people from China

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:23.270
traveling to their countries,

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:25.240
they inadvertently banned Taiwan

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:27.890
because Taiwan was seen as part of China,

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:29.343
which is what China wants.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:35.690
- Mm-hm, okay, so the WHO drops the ball,

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:38.440
and then we've subsequently seen

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:40.370
some other strange tweets and we've seen

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:44.613
some strange statements by
some of their representatives.

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:48.290
Trump was talking about a ban from China

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:49.803
back in January, right?

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:56.320
- Yeah, he instituted the
ban I think 31st of January,

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:00.190
that's when the ban initially happened.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:02.170
But, at the time, the WHO was saying

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:04.080
that they shouldn't do it,

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.720
that you shouldn't
restrict travel to China.

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:10.163
And shortly after that, also
the Chinese Foreign Ministry,

00:18:11.333 --> 00:18:13.470
they reacted very badly to Trump's ban,

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:17.143
saying that it was racist and xenophobic

00:18:17.143 --> 00:18:18.760
to institute the ban.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:19.890
- Yeah, surprise, surprise.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:22.260
So what do you make of the fact that now,

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:24.617
because hindsight is
20/20, people are saying,

00:18:24.617 --> 00:18:27.807
"Oh Trump didn't do anything in February.

00:18:27.807 --> 00:18:29.617
"Everyone knew that this was gonna happen.

00:18:29.617 --> 00:18:30.900
"Trump didn't do anything."

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:32.900
I checked yesterday and it turns out

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:34.710
that there was a Democratic debate

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:38.300
on February, I think
it's 7th, 19th and 25th,

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:39.830
none of those debates mentioned

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:41.560
anything about coronavirus, oddly,

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:43.330
yet the media is now telling us

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:44.700
that everybody was talking about

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:46.680
all of this stuff in February.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:47.947
- Oh, no they were not.

00:18:48.890 --> 00:18:51.890
- Could he have done more?
Should he have done more?

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:54.590
How do you balance keeping people sane

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:56.890
and keeping the economy open versus

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:58.933
a potential looming threat, all of that.

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:03.030
- I mean he did suggest that
he was somewhat distracted

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:05.040
by the impeachment proceedings

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:06.710
and for the whole month of February,

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:10.760
you did have our journalist
class doing one of two things,

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:14.030
saying that this was just another flu

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:21.660
or warning people that the
stigma against Asian-Americans

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:24.160
was worse than the virus itself.

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:26.470
And so even on the 9th of February,

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:29.340
you had the health
officials in New York City

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:32.820
saying come out for our
Lunar New Year Parade,

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:35.550
everything's okay, life is normal,

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.370
stand up to xenophobia and racism.

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:40.970
So they were still acting
as if, understandably,

00:19:40.970 --> 00:19:42.493
a lot of people missed this,

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:46.800
it's okay, let's all just party and mix

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:51.800
and stand in defiance to this virus.

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:56.070
So a lot of people missed
it but I read yesterday

00:19:56.070 --> 00:20:00.610
that on the 29th of
January that Peter Navarro,

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:03.673
who was the trade official
in Trump's cabinet,

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:07.080
actually wrote a memo warning and saying

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.710
that this virus is the real deal

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:13.170
and there's gonna be, at
worst, two million deaths,

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:16.240
at best a hundred thousand
but we gotta act on this.

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:19.070
And he proposed actually a travel ban.

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:20.580
Trump said he didn't read the memo

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:22.580
but shortly after, on the 31st,

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:25.910
he actually did put down the travel ban.

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:28.670
That's one of the good things about Trump,

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:30.920
is that he didn't really care about that

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:34.600
and this virus really fit in well

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:37.660
with his ideological priors for being

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:41.240
conservative on immigration and being

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:45.790
a bit more nationalistic in
terms of trade and engagement.

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:46.800
America first.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.790
- Yeah, can you talk a little bit about

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:52.780
just the inner machinations of China?

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:55.700
Because right now, just
in the last few days,

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:57.820
we've seen this thing where U.S. cases

00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:01.810
are spiking but China is
reporting no new cases,

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:04.820
no new deaths, and it's like, all right.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:07.810
And then NBC News will
literally make that statement.

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:09.400
The AP will make that statement.

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:12.030
And it's like, well I guess
that's sort of reporting,

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:14.100
in that you're reporting
what China's reporting

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:16.660
but that's not really
independent reporting.

00:21:16.660 --> 00:21:18.940
Can you talk a little
bit about how information

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:22.280
can or can't travel in China?

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:24.700
- The media's job is to be skeptical

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:27.223
and, towards that end,
they are very skeptical

00:21:27.223 --> 00:21:30.140
when it comes to reporting
about the Trump administration

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:34.340
but they're not affording
that same approach

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:36.860
to the Chinese government,
when of all people

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:38.970
who you should be skeptical about,

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:40.593
it should be numbers that are coming

00:21:40.593 --> 00:21:43.793
from a very closed authoritarian society.

00:21:47.027 --> 00:21:48.920
And I think China has had a history

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:50.410
of covering up numbers in general.

00:21:50.410 --> 00:21:54.020
People are very skeptical
about even numbers

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:57.752
coming out from their GDP calculations,

00:21:57.752 --> 00:22:00.300
how they miraculously always meet

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:04.503
the exactly 6% quota target, like exact.

00:22:05.472 --> 00:22:08.107
So they have a history of hiding things

00:22:08.107 --> 00:22:11.010
and the fact that they had no qualms

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:13.200
suppressing the whistleblower,

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.623
censoring their social media.

00:22:18.226 --> 00:22:20.220
Why would we not assume that

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:23.223
they're covering up their numbers?

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:26.980
- So if we can't trust the numbers

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:28.340
that we're getting out of China,

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:30.640
and, sadly, we can't really trust

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:32.650
our own media to report on it properly

00:22:32.650 --> 00:22:34.810
rather than just regurgitate the numbers,

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:39.130
how do we get some sensible
information out of China?

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:41.580
Is there a way to do it
that you can truly trust?

00:22:42.970 --> 00:22:47.890
- Well the CIA apparently they submitted

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:51.140
a classified report to the White House,

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:52.603
Bloomberg reported on it,

00:22:53.750 --> 00:22:55.443
and they actually basically said that

00:22:55.443 --> 00:22:57.880
they were trying to verify China's numbers

00:22:57.880 --> 00:22:59.640
and they were unable to.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:03.460
So apart from intelligence agencies

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:07.510
or using some sort of proxy,

00:23:07.510 --> 00:23:10.200
I think a lot of people saw these videos

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.980
that were circulated on social media,

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:16.483
all the urns that were
going to funeral homes,

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:22.560
and it didn't seem to match
up with what was reported.

00:23:24.593 --> 00:23:25.720
The problem is that a lot of these videos,

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:27.830
once they get posted
by citizen journalists

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:29.776
on the ground in Wuhan,

00:23:29.776 --> 00:23:32.730
they end up getting taken down so fast.

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:36.980
So the information out of
China's just unreliable

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:41.380
and, unfortunately, the only
thing that we have to work with

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:43.630
is the numbers they're
submitting to the WHO.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:47.400
- Yeah, can you talk a little bit more

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:50.970
just generally about our
relationship with China,

00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:54.100
China's sort of geopolitical ambitions

00:23:54.100 --> 00:23:56.400
and some of the stuff
that they've been doing

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:00.303
that has kind of led us to
this really precarious point?

00:24:01.190 --> 00:24:05.390
- Well, China's been on the
ascending for a long time,

00:24:05.390 --> 00:24:08.903
ever since Deng Xiaoping
instituted market reforms.

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:12.190
They insist on calling it socialism

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:14.100
with Chinese characteristics

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:15.540
and that's really just a euphemism

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:18.080
for moving it in a more
capitalistic direction

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:22.140
and it's been slowly
liberalizing economically.

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:26.870
And we've engaged with
China since Richard Nixon

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:29.260
went in and built that relationship.

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:33.900
And every president so
far has engaged with them.

00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:35.480
We've sort of subscribed to this idea

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:38.880
that the more interconnected
our markets are

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:41.900
and the richer China becomes,

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:45.230
eventually it's going to
liberalize politically as well,

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:47.613
that you'll see freedoms come in.

00:24:47.613 --> 00:24:51.230
That the average person in
China who's more affluent

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:53.760
will start demanding to
have freedom of speech

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:58.010
and political freedoms to
vote their leaders out.

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:00.630
And that just hasn't been the case at all.

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:04.060
And, if anything, China's
become far more powerful

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:07.370
than they've been and they're also growing

00:25:07.370 --> 00:25:08.470
in their ambitions.

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:11.480
So you can see this with
their military expansions

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:15.010
in the South China Sea, the
Belt and Road Initiative,

00:25:15.010 --> 00:25:18.370
which is basically this
infrastructure project

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:20.870
where they sign up all these countries

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:23.360
around the world to build infrastructure

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:28.297
and that becomes what they
call debt-trap diplomacy.

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:32.970
So they're holding them in a way

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:34.870
and using that as their economic clout

00:25:36.369 --> 00:25:38.850
as a way to have leverage
on these countries.

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:42.670
So the Chinese system
is really not compatible

00:25:42.670 --> 00:25:47.670
with the U.S.'s vision for
the world and also values.

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:49.980
And, I think, it's quite clear

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:53.400
that we're becoming
strategic rivals and that,

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.360
in fact, there's a good
argument to be made

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:58.540
also that China is
posing as a very possible

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:00.543
existential threat to the United States.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.750
- So are you saying that
for the two plus years

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:07.660
that we were focused
on Russia and Ukraine,

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:09.600
perhaps we should've
been focused elsewhere

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:11.980
and that our eye was off the ball

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:13.830
because of some other nonsense,

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:15.460
is that what you're getting at?

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:18.670
- Well, you know, one of
Trump's campaign promises

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:22.560
was, he has been talking
about China a lot,

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:25.840
you've seen these clips, like
China, when he does that.

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:29.750
That's just a central
issue to his platform

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:32.050
and he's been talking
about it for a long time,

00:26:32.050 --> 00:26:34.320
that we've got a very
bad trade deal with them

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:36.740
and he wants to come in and fix it.

00:26:36.740 --> 00:26:40.310
The problem with his rhetoric was that he

00:26:40.310 --> 00:26:41.460
sort of talked about it in a sense

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:44.130
of making America strong
again economically,

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:49.130
bringing jobs home, but he didn't frame it

00:26:49.270 --> 00:26:52.650
in terms of this geopolitical
struggle, which it is.

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:56.730
Maybe that's because of
the need to still have some

00:26:56.730 --> 00:27:00.600
form of relationship in
real politic with China

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:02.400
and that would really offend them.

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:06.900
But all China analysts and people

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:09.083
who have been on the ball on this issue,

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:12.430
have been sounding the alarm for a while.

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:16.230
Look at how they're able to coerce us,

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:18.648
even outside of their own borders,

00:27:18.648 --> 00:27:22.620
to basically parrot their orthodoxy

00:27:22.620 --> 00:27:27.620
when it comes to the whole
NBA Hong Kong fiasco,

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:32.660
or even what kind of messages
are embedded in movies.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:38.083
So you can see they've been
trying to influence from afar.

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:43.660
- How dangerous is it that
we owe them a lot of money?

00:27:43.660 --> 00:27:45.770
These are our creditors.

00:27:45.770 --> 00:27:48.180
I'm pretty sure we're never
gonna pay that money back.

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:49.330
Where does that put us?

00:27:50.637 --> 00:27:53.740
- In a very precarious position.

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:55.710
But there are also very strong signs

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:59.133
that the Chinese economy
itself is not very sustainable.

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:01.750
And that there are issues,

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:05.483
that we're just waiting
for this bubble to pop.

00:28:09.852 --> 00:28:12.340
So being indebted to
China obviously is giving

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:15.140
us a lot of issues when
it comes to leverage

00:28:15.140 --> 00:28:17.000
and it's also a little
bit of an embarrassment

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:19.090
that the world superpower is unable

00:28:19.090 --> 00:28:24.090
to have that financial
hold over a trading partner

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:26.653
that's also a strategic rival.

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:32.290
But Trump has actually
ballooned that trait,

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:34.700
our deficit, since he came into office.

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:38.310
And the way this crisis is going to go,

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:42.373
that's just gonna get even
worse with our economy.

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:45.620
- Yeah, and it's not just our economy

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:47.370
and our government
that's in bed with China.

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:50.840
We've got the NBA in bed with China,

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:54.010
we've got universities in bed with China,

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:58.260
giant corporations, so
there's a multilayered

00:28:58.260 --> 00:28:59.513
element to this, right?

00:28:59.513 --> 00:29:00.640
- (microphone distorts) companies too.

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:03.010
You talked about NBC just now, right.

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:04.770
NBC's parent company, Comcast,

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:07.640
actually had (microphone
distorts) deals in China.

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.250
They've invested billions of dollars

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:13.230
to build a Minion Land, this theme park.

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:15.400
I can't think of anything worse.

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:17.830
To me, that is literally hell,

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:22.363
it's being in Minion Land in Beijing.

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:25.740
So they've invested billions
in these theme parks,

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:27.370
Harry Potter Village.

00:29:27.370 --> 00:29:32.370
And you can just imagine
what China did to the NBA,

00:29:33.560 --> 00:29:37.400
after one general manager said
something about Hong Kong,

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:40.410
where they just canceled
all the screenings

00:29:40.410 --> 00:29:42.250
of the NBA in China.

00:29:42.250 --> 00:29:44.580
That was how they retaliated
when they didn't like

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:47.620
what one NBA manager tweeted.

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:50.607
Can you imagine the kind of leverage

00:29:50.607 --> 00:29:53.920
over media companies,
over Hollywood companies

00:29:53.920 --> 00:29:56.403
because they have business
dealings with them?

00:29:57.520 --> 00:29:59.320
- What do you make of
the way that everyone

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:01.390
falls into line when it comes to China?

00:30:01.390 --> 00:30:04.040
So that NBA thing was pretty amazing

00:30:04.040 --> 00:30:06.000
because it was a random general manager,

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:07.250
I think for the Houston Rockets,

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:09.520
or maybe it was even an
assistant general manager,

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:11.210
but a guy that nobody really knew his name

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:12.933
outside of the, yeah,

00:30:13.987 --> 00:30:16.110
so he is the general manager.

00:30:16.110 --> 00:30:17.490
But nobody really knows his name

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:19.730
outside of the basketball world.

00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:23.720
But after that happened,
the amount of NBA players,

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:27.710
Lebron James included, who
all sort of bowed to China,

00:30:27.710 --> 00:30:29.940
the same people who will go on and on

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:32.400
and rip America as a racist, evil state

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:34.380
and tell you that Trump is Hitler,

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:36.530
but immediately, all of them,

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:39.340
the entire league, suddenly was bowing.

00:30:39.340 --> 00:30:41.290
Steve Kerr is a great example of that,

00:30:41.290 --> 00:30:43.220
who runs around saying how awful

00:30:43.220 --> 00:30:44.370
Trump is and everything else.

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:45.770
But suddenly, when it came to China,

00:30:45.770 --> 00:30:48.203
ooh, gotta shut up and quick.

00:30:49.060 --> 00:30:51.900
- Well, the Chinese market is much larger

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:53.930
than the U.S. market for the NBA.

00:30:53.930 --> 00:30:57.250
And they've been trying to get
into China for a long time.

00:30:57.250 --> 00:31:00.860
So this has been, for them,
just the biggest moneymaker.

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:02.820
Not to mention all the licensing deals,

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:06.083
NBA stores there, shoe
companies and all that.

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:09.060
I would say there's actually one player

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:11.350
that's been pretty good
on this China issue

00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:14.610
and that is Enes Kanter, and
that's because he himself

00:31:14.610 --> 00:31:17.910
knows what it's like to
live under an authoritarian

00:31:17.910 --> 00:31:20.660
and what it's like to stand up to one,

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:23.140
Turkish prime minister.
- Turkish in this case, yeah.

00:31:23.140 --> 00:31:24.866
- Yeah, exactly.

00:31:24.866 --> 00:31:29.866
So it's a shame that market access alone

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:32.530
can really modify our behavior so much,

00:31:32.530 --> 00:31:35.150
to the point where these players

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:37.873
who are so quick to support causes.

00:31:39.305 --> 00:31:41.280
There was a NBA boycott of North Carolina

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:43.030
because of the bathroom bills

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:46.792
that when it came to
sticking up for our values,

00:31:46.792 --> 00:31:50.330
for the right of Americans,

00:31:50.330 --> 00:31:53.240
who just have an opinion about Hong Kong,

00:31:53.240 --> 00:31:55.770
all of a sudden it just
went out the window.

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:56.890
That's really sad.

00:31:56.890 --> 00:31:59.300
- Yeah, by the way, what's
the latest in Hong Kong?

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:00.770
I mean, with everything
going on right now,

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:03.440
we don't hear anything
about Hong Kong anymore.

00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:04.600
But, I mean, they were singing

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:06.810
the national anthem on the streets.

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:08.200
- Yeah, yeah, but there's

00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:10.330
- American national anthem.

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:12.410
- And they were waving the flags

00:32:12.410 --> 00:32:14.263
and also waving the Union Jack.

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:17.640
Well, the coronavirus, obviously,

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:21.700
has rendered all protests impossible.

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:25.120
So, in that sense, it's
really muted that down.

00:32:25.120 --> 00:32:30.120
And also, recently, they
had this anti-masking law,

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:33.640
the Chief Executive of Hong Kong,

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:35.490
because they didn't want their protestors

00:32:35.490 --> 00:32:37.840
to be able to mask up but now,

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:40.700
because everybody
actually has to wear masks

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:42.960
to protect themselves
against the coronavirus,

00:32:42.960 --> 00:32:44.190
they had to rewrite the law.

00:32:44.190 --> 00:32:47.160
So that's the last I heard about Hong Kong

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:49.170
but there are no protests going on now.

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:53.210
And it's a shame because it's so moving

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:55.630
to see young people on the streets,

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:57.610
standing up against tyranny,

00:32:57.610 --> 00:32:59.483
standing up against authoritarianism.

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:04.730
- What's your sense that
once we get past this thing,

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:08.090
which I assume you
believe we'll get past it,

00:33:08.090 --> 00:33:10.360
how some of the nature of the relationship

00:33:10.360 --> 00:33:12.740
with China will Change?

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:15.453
Or at least our discussion
about what they're doing?

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:20.263
- I think, in a way, America
has woken up to this.

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:22.980
It shouldn't have taken this.

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:27.980
But we've woken up to the
fact that our industrial

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:32.950
supply chain was located
inside an adversary.

00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:37.130
Our medical, lifesaving medical drugs,

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:39.580
all the active ingredients in our drugs

00:33:39.580 --> 00:33:42.453
were also made in China.

00:33:43.826 --> 00:33:46.660
And just people are starting to see

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:50.590
and wake up to this fact
that there is a clash

00:33:50.590 --> 00:33:53.180
in value systems between
the two countries.

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:56.570
And it just couldn't be more clear now.

00:33:56.570 --> 00:34:01.290
It should've been clear to us,
what they did with the NBA,

00:34:01.290 --> 00:34:03.790
the Hong Kong issue, it
should've been clear then.

00:34:04.676 --> 00:34:07.740
But now it's rally hitting us.

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:10.987
And I've seen a lot of
people now express the view,

00:34:10.987 --> 00:34:13.587
"You know what, I don't care
about the cheap stuff anymore.

00:34:13.587 --> 00:34:17.367
"I'd rather just pay more and be secure

00:34:17.367 --> 00:34:21.650
"and have our supply chains
relocated back to America."

00:34:21.650 --> 00:34:23.280
Especially when it comes
to the sensitive stuff.

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:26.380
I'm not talking about
making crayons or something,

00:34:26.380 --> 00:34:31.380
we're talking about
anything to do with tech,

00:34:31.490 --> 00:34:34.963
the higher value items.

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:39.660
- Yeah, so moving away
from China a little bit,

00:34:39.660 --> 00:34:42.170
for our remaining time, you're
a pretty astute observer

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:43.890
of what's happening in America too

00:34:43.890 --> 00:34:46.640
and I know you've sort of been on your own

00:34:46.640 --> 00:34:48.730
political journey over
the last couple of years,

00:34:48.730 --> 00:34:51.020
that I think in some ways
is sort of similar to mine,

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:52.540
even though we come, obviously,

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:54.710
from a very a different starting point.

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:56.330
What do you make about what's happening

00:34:56.330 --> 00:34:58.240
right now politically in America?

00:34:58.240 --> 00:35:00.740
The way the conversation is?

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:02.830
As we're taping this
today, we just found out

00:35:02.830 --> 00:35:04.550
that Bernie Sanders is dropping out.

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:06.350
We're holding this for a little bit.

00:35:07.281 --> 00:35:10.010
Do you think Biden can even do this?

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.470
Where are you at with Trump?

00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:13.450
What's your general take,

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:15.540
as the New York editor
of "Spectator" magazine,

00:35:15.540 --> 00:35:18.680
which is actually one of
the few places of journalism

00:35:18.680 --> 00:35:20.230
that I don't have to
use air quotes around,

00:35:20.230 --> 00:35:21.980
which is a really refreshing thing.

00:35:23.290 --> 00:35:25.380
- Yeah, well, I'm really excited

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:26.900
that they gave me an opportunity

00:35:26.900 --> 00:35:31.210
and they've never tried to muzzle me

00:35:31.210 --> 00:35:35.540
or steer me into an ideological track.

00:35:35.540 --> 00:35:36.910
It's just write whatever you want,

00:35:36.910 --> 00:35:39.000
pitch whatever you want,
and so, in that sense,

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:41.750
I do have a lot of journalistic freedom.

00:35:41.750 --> 00:35:44.380
In terms of where America is politically,

00:35:44.380 --> 00:35:49.380
this coronavirus issue really
threw a wrench into it.

00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:51.880
We were all kind of going along

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:54.900
and all of the sudden, the
news just changed overnight.

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:56.410
Just completely saturated.

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:58.354
And, at this point, it's not even clear

00:35:58.354 --> 00:36:01.070
if things are going to get lifted.

00:36:01.070 --> 00:36:04.220
I'm hearing conversations now about voting

00:36:04.220 --> 00:36:07.483
and whether we're gonna do
this mail voting at some point.

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:11.770
I don't know but, suffice to say,

00:36:11.770 --> 00:36:15.703
there is a lot of
dissatisfaction about Joe Biden.

00:36:16.580 --> 00:36:19.770
And I wrote a piece for
"Spectator" about how

00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:23.000
I don't think the media
actually vetted him properly.

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:25.920
I don't think they put
him through the gauntlet

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:29.240
in a way that they did do
to every other candidate.

00:36:29.240 --> 00:36:32.880
If you looked at Pete
Buttigieg, Bloomberg,

00:36:32.880 --> 00:36:34.730
even Andrew Yang, you
would find a hit piece

00:36:34.730 --> 00:36:35.940
on "The New York Times" about how they

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:38.640
were either racist in
their past or sexist,

00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:40.270
except maybe Pete Buttigieg.

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:43.038
But there's something there.

00:36:43.038 --> 00:36:46.170
And Biden just wasn't put
through the same pressures.

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:49.690
And so he's emerged and
now the big question

00:36:49.690 --> 00:36:54.690
is about his mental
state, does he have the,

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:58.780
not just temperament but is he sane enough

00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:02.630
to actually be the president
of the United States.

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:06.650
And what he's coming up against Trump,

00:37:06.650 --> 00:37:09.850
the optics, it's gonna look so bad.

00:37:09.850 --> 00:37:12.250
But the issue is, at the end of the day,

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:14.470
the math just didn't work out for Bernie.

00:37:14.470 --> 00:37:16.730
I know a lot of people liked him.

00:37:16.730 --> 00:37:19.910
I mean, your and my friend now, I guess,

00:37:19.910 --> 00:37:21.700
Joe Rogan really liked Bernie too.

00:37:21.700 --> 00:37:25.320
But I see a lot of people
kind of echoing what he said,

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:29.500
which is "Bernie or bust" on the left.

00:37:29.500 --> 00:37:32.180
And it's creating such a big rift.

00:37:32.180 --> 00:37:34.330
I don't know how that's
gonna get resolved.

00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:38.350
- Yeah, so as someone
that's now in the media,

00:37:38.350 --> 00:37:40.890
what do you make of the media these days?

00:37:40.890 --> 00:37:44.470
Because I'm sort of like, man.

00:37:44.470 --> 00:37:46.420
You know, a lot of us have
been screaming about the media

00:37:46.420 --> 00:37:48.790
for a while and I think
maybe now that so many people

00:37:48.790 --> 00:37:52.020
are trapped at home and
they're forced online more,

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:53.950
I think it's becoming
more and more obvious

00:37:53.950 --> 00:37:56.430
to people that maybe weren't
taking the temperature.

00:37:56.430 --> 00:37:58.160
So that if you were watching CNN

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:00.690
with their selectively edited Trump quotes

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:02.970
and normally you wouldn't pay
that much attention to it,

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:04.450
now you're stuck at home online

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:07.580
and you're seeing full quotes be shared

00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:10.200
by other people where
they stop his sentence

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:13.103
right after he says but
this da-da-da-da-dah.

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:17.470
Do you sense that the media
has just gone completely crazy?

00:38:17.470 --> 00:38:19.300
Is there a way to get them to come back?

00:38:19.300 --> 00:38:22.230
I mean can "The New York
Times" reel it back in?

00:38:22.230 --> 00:38:23.630
Can CNN reel it back in?

00:38:23.630 --> 00:38:26.610
Or have they just chosen a
model that they think is best,

00:38:26.610 --> 00:38:29.000
which is, well, I guess, lie,

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:30.650
because that's what your base wants?

00:38:30.650 --> 00:38:31.650
Something like that?

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:38.040
- Well, I saw that CBS was
using video clips of Italy

00:38:38.455 --> 00:38:41.170
when they were talking
about the New York situation

00:38:41.170 --> 00:38:43.660
and that is just gaslighting.

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:45.490
- Italian hospitals, they were literally

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:47.153
showing Italian hospitals.

00:38:49.220 --> 00:38:50.700
- It does erode the public trust.

00:38:50.700 --> 00:38:51.870
I mean, I think we have to separate

00:38:51.870 --> 00:38:54.933
between the opinion pieces
and the actual reporting.

00:38:56.450 --> 00:38:59.670
The International Bureau
of these big organizations,

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:01.850
like "The New York Times",
they do excellent reporting

00:39:01.850 --> 00:39:03.740
when it comes to the Middle East,

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:06.777
I mean, even on China,
which recently expelled

00:39:06.777 --> 00:39:08.750
"Wall Street Journal", "Washington Post"

00:39:08.750 --> 00:39:09.970
and "The New York Times" journalists

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:13.070
that were there for decades,
expelled out of Beijing.

00:39:13.070 --> 00:39:15.759
And now we don't have eyes on them there.

00:39:15.759 --> 00:39:19.160
They have opinion pieces
and activist journalists,

00:39:19.160 --> 00:39:22.380
that's really where I
think the problem is.

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:26.820
And, apart from just the
business model issue,

00:39:26.820 --> 00:39:29.210
which I think we haven't
really solved yet.

00:39:29.210 --> 00:39:34.210
You've actually kind of
built a platform to solve it.

00:39:35.010 --> 00:39:38.930
But that is one of the
reasons why this world

00:39:38.930 --> 00:39:40.960
of activism journalism exists.

00:39:40.960 --> 00:39:43.820
It's click baity and the incentives

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:46.370
that reward that kind of
journalism is still there.

00:39:47.889 --> 00:39:51.020
I don't know how to solve this.

00:39:51.020 --> 00:39:53.300
It's just, apart from changing this

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:55.840
and rewiring the whole
incentive structure,

00:39:55.840 --> 00:39:57.960
or just having what we do have now,

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:00.010
this fragmentation of media,

00:40:00.010 --> 00:40:02.010
is actually probably a good thing.

00:40:02.010 --> 00:40:04.172
I know we used to say
that it was a bad thing.

00:40:04.172 --> 00:40:06.600
But the fact that there
is conservative media,

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:09.810
I mean, frankly, Tucker Carlson,
I'm so impressed with him.

00:40:09.810 --> 00:40:11.020
The coverage that he has done

00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:13.200
since this coronavirus started.

00:40:13.200 --> 00:40:14.100
And actually before that,

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:16.800
he was doing really good
stuff also on China.

00:40:16.800 --> 00:40:20.110
But it's important more
than ever that we do

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:23.000
have these voices because
it puts the pressure.

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:24.890
I had a tweet about this the other day.

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:28.450
So there was a clip where
a Hong Kong journalist,

00:40:28.450 --> 00:40:32.130
interviewed someone from the
WHO, a very senior official,

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:35.070
and asked him about Taiwan
and he just backtracked.

00:40:35.070 --> 00:40:38.240
It was such a shameful, tried to weasel

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:41.970
out of the conversation
and it looked really bad.

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:43.980
And for the first four hours,

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:45.357
the only media that reported on it

00:40:45.357 --> 00:40:48.670
was the Blaze, was Fox News, was PJ Media,

00:40:48.670 --> 00:40:51.130
was all the so-called right wing.

00:40:51.130 --> 00:40:52.790
But if it didn't get picked up by them,

00:40:52.790 --> 00:40:55.160
the story would've never bubbled up.

00:40:55.160 --> 00:40:58.200
And by picking up on it, they were forcing

00:40:59.200 --> 00:41:02.530
the mainstream media to
respond, to log that,

00:41:02.530 --> 00:41:04.460
and it kind of gets into
the public consciousness

00:41:04.460 --> 00:41:06.600
in a way that, imagine
if they didn't exist,

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:07.960
it just wouldn't have.

00:41:07.960 --> 00:41:12.960
So it is important that
we have competing interest

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:15.853
and at least there's a chance
that the truth gets out.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:19.910
- Yeah and I know for
you, as I think someone

00:41:19.910 --> 00:41:23.030
that you still consider
yourself a good, decent Liberal,

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:25.380
it's kinda funny that
you have all these allies

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:26.700
on the right and you have to give props

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:29.500
to conservative media, I suppose,

00:41:29.500 --> 00:41:30.600
to get these stories out there.

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:32.600
Unfortunately, we don't have
anymore time but quickly

00:41:32.600 --> 00:41:34.070
in 30 seconds, and then next time

00:41:34.070 --> 00:41:35.870
we're obviously gonna do this in person

00:41:35.870 --> 00:41:37.770
and it will be great to see you again.

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:42.120
Just give me your sense
of when we get out of this

00:41:42.120 --> 00:41:45.173
and are we gonna be okay on
the other side of this thing?

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:47.940
- I think we're gonna have
to learn how to live with it,

00:41:47.940 --> 00:41:49.510
as long as there is no vaccine,

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:53.220
as long as whatever antiviral treatments

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:55.880
are still not developed,
we're gonna have to learn

00:41:55.880 --> 00:41:58.530
how to live with the virus
and there's gonna be ways.

00:41:59.391 --> 00:42:02.661
So we're in mitigation stage now

00:42:02.661 --> 00:42:06.390
and I hope our curves start to flatten.

00:42:06.390 --> 00:42:09.690
California's doing a
very good job with it.

00:42:09.690 --> 00:42:13.460
So we'll see, but things are realigning

00:42:13.460 --> 00:42:15.750
and lives are changing.

00:42:15.750 --> 00:42:17.470
- All right, Melissa,
it was great seeing you,

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:19.410
even though we had to do this
through the Skype machine.

00:42:19.410 --> 00:42:21.420
